		================
		** SYSBLD.DOC **
		================



The following instructions are designed to help you build a
63K CP/M system.


STEP #1. Use MOVCPM to create a COM file with the basic CP/M
system in it. Call it CPM63.COM.


STEP #2. Alter RMSIOS to fit your specific hardware configuration.
Assemble RMSIOS and produce an object file RMSIOS.HEX.


STEP #3. Repeat STEP #2 for RMSBOOT and RMSPUT.


STEP #4. Put all the pieces together to form a complete system.
Save that system on your diskette. It is done thusly:

	A>DDT CPM63.COM		;Load DDT and BDOS
	DDT VERS 2.2
	-IRMSPUT.HEX
	-R			;Read RMSPUT into RAM at 100H
	-IRMSBOOT.HEX
	-R880			;Read RMSBOOT into RAM at 880H
	-IRMSIOS.HEX
	-R2980			;Read RMSIOS into RAM at 1F80H
	-G0			;Warm Boot CP/M
	A>SAVE 38 RMS63.COM	;Save the system on your diskette


STEP #5. Assemble the disk format program RMSFMT. Use it to format
ths disk drive.


STEP #6. Write the system onto the systems area of the drive. It is
done like this:

	A>RMS62


STEP #7. Boot the system and test it.





NOTES
-----

1. Because of the size of RMSIOS, the system requires an additional
   1K bytes of memory. In other words, if you have 64k of memory you'll
   need to build a 63K system.

2. For those familiar with CP/M V1.4, the SYSGEN memory layout for BOOT,
   BDOS, & CBIOS has changed. BOOT starts 80H bytes lower in RAM because
   the sector size on the hard disk is 256 bytes in length (as opposed to
   a floppy disk which has a sector length of 128 bytes).  CBIOS starts
   100H bytes higher in RAM because BDOS has grown.

		BOOT	BDOS	CBIOS

	1.4	900H	980H	1E80H
	2.2	880H	980H	1F80H
